    'body'        => <<<'HTML'
<p>If you are wondering what to do after a house fire, start here: get everyone to safety, call your insurance company the same day, and document everything before you clean up or throw anything away. The choices you make in the first week shape how quickly you get back into a safe home and how much of your claim gets paid.</p>
<p>This checklist walks Texas homeowners through the first 24 hours, the insurance claim, temporary housing, contents inventories and the payout math that trips up a lot of families.</p>

<h2>The first 24 hours after a house fire</h2>
<p>Your priority is people, not property. Once the fire is out, the next few hours are about safety, shelter and a few calls that protect your claim.</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Stay out until you are cleared.</strong> Do not re-enter until the fire department says it is safe. Floors, ceilings and stairs can be weakened, and hidden embers can flare up again.</li>
<li><strong>Get medical attention.</strong> Smoke inhalation and burns are not always obvious right away. Get anyone with coughing, dizziness or breathing trouble checked.</li>
<li><strong>Find a safe place to sleep tonight.</strong> Family and friends are the first call. If you have nowhere to go, the American Red Cross (1-800-RED-CROSS) often responds to home fires with help for immediate needs such as lodging, food and replacement medication.</li>
<li><strong>Leave utilities alone.</strong> The fire department may shut off gas, water or electricity. Do not turn them back on yourself. In the Houston area, call your electric and gas providers to have service checked before it is restored.</li>
<li><strong>Call your insurance company or agent.</strong> Report the loss, get a claim number, and ask about emergency funds for living expenses.</li>
<li><strong>Ask for the fire report.</strong> Get the fire department's name, the incident number and how to request a copy. Your insurer will want it.</li>
</ol>

<h2>Notify your insurer and understand the claim timeline</h2>
<p>Report the fire as soon as you can, and write down the date, time and name of everyone you speak with. Ask for the adjuster's contact information and a copy of your full policy, not just the declarations page.</p>
<p>Texas has a Prompt Payment of Claims law in Chapter 542 of the Insurance Code. In general, a licensed insurer has 15 days after receiving notice of a claim to acknowledge it, start investigating and request the information it needs. Once it has everything it asked for, it generally has 15 business days to accept or reject the claim in writing, and it must explain any rejection. If it needs more time, it must tell you why, and then it has up to 45 more days. If the insurer accepts the claim, payment is generally due within five business days after it notifies you.</p>
<p>Fire claims carry one special rule: if an insurer has a reasonable basis to suspect arson, the decision window is 30 days instead of 15 business days. That is not an accusation, and many fires are investigated simply to rule causes out. Cooperate, answer questions honestly, and keep copies of everything you submit. The Texas Department of Insurance explains these deadlines on its <a href="https://tdi.texas.gov/blog/insurance-claim-deadlines.html">insurance claim deadlines page</a>.</p>

<h2>Additional living expenses: who pays for your hotel</h2>
<p>Most homeowners policies include loss of use coverage, often called additional living expenses (ALE). If the fire makes your home unlivable, ALE pays the <em>increase</em> in your normal costs of living while repairs happen.</p>
<p>That can include a hotel or short-term rental, extra mileage to work or school, laundry, pet boarding and the difference between your usual grocery bill and eating out. It does not pay for costs you would have had anyway, like your mortgage payment.</p>
<ul>
<li>Check your declarations page for the ALE limit. It may be a dollar cap, a time limit or both.</li>
<li>Ask your adjuster for an advance so you are not floating hotel costs on a credit card.</li>
<li>Keep every receipt and a simple log of daily expenses. Compare against what you normally spend.</li>
<li>If repairs will take months, ask about a furnished rental. It is often cheaper than a hotel and easier on a family.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Document the damage before anything is cleaned</h2>
<p>Good documentation is the difference between a smooth claim and a long argument. Once you are allowed back inside, take photos and video of every room before you move or clean anything.</p>
<ul>
<li>Walk each room slowly with video, narrating what you see. Open closets, cabinets and drawers.</li>
<li>Photograph ceilings, floors, HVAC vents and outlets. Smoke and soot travel far beyond the burned area.</li>
<li>Shoot wide shots for context, then close-ups of serial numbers, brand labels and model plates.</li>
<li>Do not throw away damaged items until your adjuster says you can. If something is a health hazard, photograph it first.</li>
<li>Keep a claim journal: every call, email, visit and promise, with dates and names.</li>
</ul>
<p>A cloud backup of photos and receipts protects you if a phone is lost. A scanner makes it easier to send clean copies of estimates and receipts to your adjuster.</p>
[[product:document-scanner]]

<h2>Build a contents inventory room by room</h2>
<p>Your personal property claim usually requires a written inventory listing each item, when you bought it, what you paid and what it would cost to replace today. It is tedious, and it is where many families leave money on the table.</p>
<p>Work one room at a time. Use your photos and videos, then jog your memory with old social media posts, online shopping order histories, credit card statements and store loyalty accounts. Ask family members to list what was in their own rooms. Do not forget the garage, attic, pantry, linens, cleaning supplies and holiday decorations.</p>
<p>A simple spreadsheet works well. Use columns for room, item, brand and model, quantity, age, original price, replacement cost and condition. Your insurer may provide its own form, so ask before you start.</p>

<h2>Mitigation, board-up and avoiding storm chasers</h2>
<p>Most policies require you to take reasonable steps to protect the property from further damage. After a fire that usually means boarding up windows and doors, tarping the roof, securing the property against theft and starting water removal if firefighting soaked the house. Ask your insurer before you hire anyone, keep receipts, and photograph the work. Reasonable mitigation costs are typically part of the claim.</p>
<p>Expect people to show up quickly offering to handle everything. Some are legitimate. Others pressure homeowners into signing contracts on the spot. Before you sign anything:</p>
<ul>
<li>Get at least two written estimates and check reviews and references.</li>
<li>Read every page. Be wary of contracts that assign your insurance benefits to the contractor.</li>
<li>Refuse anyone who offers to waive your deductible. TDI notes that it is illegal in Texas for a contractor to offer to waive, rebate or absorb a policyholder's deductible.</li>
<li>Know that a contractor cannot also act as your public adjuster on the same job in Texas.</li>
<li>Never pay large sums upfront in cash.</li>
</ul>
<p>If you want an advocate on your side, read our guide to <a href="/blog/public-adjuster-vs-insurance-adjuster/">public adjusters vs insurance adjusters</a> before signing with anyone.</p>

<h2>Recovering and replacing important documents</h2>
<p>If deeds, IDs or financial papers were lost, start replacing them early. Many of them are needed for the claim, banking and temporary housing.</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr><th>Document</th><th>Where to start</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Driver's license or ID card</td><td>Texas Department of Public Safety</td></tr>
<tr><td>Birth and death certificates</td><td>Texas vital records office or the county clerk where the event was recorded</td></tr>
<tr><td>Social Security card</td><td>Social Security Administration</td></tr>
<tr><td>Prior tax returns and transcripts</td><td>IRS (transcripts can be requested online)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Deed and recorded documents</td><td>County clerk in the county where the property sits</td></tr>
<tr><td>Insurance policy</td><td>Your agent or insurer</td></tr>
<tr><td>Damaged cash</td><td>U.S. Bureau of Engraving and Printing, Mutilated Currency Division</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Do not throw away burned cash or savings bonds. Handle them as little as possible and ask the issuing agency how to submit them for replacement. Once you are settled, a fire-rated safe keeps replacement copies protected.</p>
[[product:sentrysafe-fireproof]]

<h2>Your mortgage lender, ACV vs RCV and recoverable depreciation</h2>
<p>If you have a mortgage, expect your lender's name on the insurance check for the dwelling. TDI says that when a mortgage company receives an insurance payment, it must contact you within 10 days, and you then work with it to release funds for repairs. Call your loan servicer early, ask for its insurance claim or loss draft department, and find out what it needs: usually a signed contractor agreement, an estimate and inspections at set stages of work. Keep paying your mortgage unless your servicer agrees to something different in writing.</p>
<p>The payout itself depends on how your policy values the loss:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Actual cash value (ACV)</strong> is the replacement cost minus depreciation for age and wear. A 10-year-old sofa is worth far less than a new one.</li>
<li><strong>Replacement cost value (RCV)</strong> is what it costs to repair or replace the item with something of like kind and quality today.</li>
</ul>
<p>Many replacement cost policies pay in two steps. First you receive the ACV. After you repair or replace the item and send proof, you can collect the depreciation that was held back, called <strong>recoverable depreciation</strong>. TDI describes this two-payment pattern on its <a href="https://tdi.texas.gov/tips/getting-your-insurance-claim-paid.html">steps to getting your claim paid</a> page. Policies often set a deadline to complete repairs and claim it, so ask your adjuster in writing.</p>
<p>To see how much depreciation is being held back on your claim, run your numbers through our <a href="/tools/insurance-claim-depreciation-calculator/">insurance claim depreciation calculator</a>.</p>
<p>If the home is too damaged to rebuild, or you would rather not, selling is an option. See <a href="/blog/sell-fire-damaged-house-texas/">how to sell a fire-damaged house in Texas</a>. For a claim dispute or a large loss, it is wise to confirm your options with a Texas attorney or licensed public adjuster.</p>

<h2>Bottom line</h2>
<p>After a house fire, put safety first, call your insurer the same day, and document every room and item before cleanup starts. Use your ALE coverage for temporary housing, keep every receipt, and build a detailed contents inventory. Know the Texas claim deadlines, loop in your mortgage lender early, and track recoverable depreciation so you collect the full replacement cost you paid for. Browse more guides in our <a href="/category/insurance-claims/">insurance claims</a> section.</p>
